            <h1 class="pb-3">Proxmox cluster</h1>

            <p>
                Three Proxmox VE hosts on the office network run the HomeLab's development workstations, home automation, cameras and media services.
                A Ryzen desktop provides most of the storage and workstation capacity, while two mini PCs run the services that are normally left on.
                Each host connects at 2.5 GbE and uses a Linux bridge, <code>vmbr0</code>, to put guest interfaces on the same LAN as the hosts.
            </p>
            <pre class="bg-light rounded-3 p-3"><code>Office network (2.5 GbE)
├── n1 · Ryzen 9 5900X, 64 GB RAM, RTX 2060
│   ├── Windows and Linux workstation VMs
│   ├── Tvheadend VM with a PCIe tuner
│   └── NAS and Proxmox Backup Server containers
├── n2 · Ryzen 7 7735HS, 32 GB RAM
│   ├── Frigate VM with GPU and USB Coral passthrough
│   └── Home Assistant OS VM with USB radio access
└── n3 · Ryzen 7 7735HS, 32 GB RAM
    ├── Plex VM
    └── Immich and media automation containers</code></pre>

            <h2 class="h4 mt-4">Cluster networking and quorum</h2>
            <p>
                Stable host addresses and name resolution give Corosync a consistent path between the three nodes. Each node has one vote, and two votes are enough to retain quorum while a third node reboots.
                Cluster membership provides a single view of the hosts and their guests in the web interface. Guest disks are primarily local, and the setup has no automatic HA failover or guest replication.
                Workloads on a host undergoing maintenance are brought back on that host after the reboot. The <a href="https://pve.proxmox.com/pve-docs/pve-admin-guide.pdf">Proxmox VE administration guide</a> covers cluster creation, quorum and Linux bridges.
            </p>
            <pre><code class="language-bash">pvecm status
pvecm nodes
ip -br link show vmbr0
qm list
pct list</code></pre>
            <p>
                The first two commands show cluster membership and quorum. The guest lists show the VMs and LXCs on the node where the commands run.
            </p>

            <h2 class="h4 mt-4">Storage and hardware dependencies</h2>
            <p>
                n1 has separate ZFS mirrors for NVMe and hard disk storage, alongside single-disk SSD pools. Its NAS container exports selected directories over SMB to Plex, Immich and the media automation services on n3.
                Tvheadend also writes recordings to a NAS share. These are application data paths. The guests still boot from their own Proxmox storage.
                The mini PCs use local LVM-thin storage for guest disks. Checking physical thin-pool usage matters because virtual disks can add up to more than the pool can hold.
            </p>
            <pre><code class="language-bash"># On a Proxmox host
pvesm status
lvs -o vg_name,lv_name,lv_size,data_percent,metadata_percent

# On n1, which hosts the ZFS pools
zpool status -x
zpool list</code></pre>

            <h3 class="h5 mt-4">Frigate and Home Assistant on n2</h3>
            <p>
                Frigate runs in Docker Compose inside a Debian VM. The VM uses the mini PC's Radeon GPU for video decoding and a USB Coral for object detection.
                A dedicated 500 GB disk is passed through to the VM, mounted at <code>/mnt/frigate_storage</code> and mapped into the container as <code>/media/frigate</code>.
                The <a href="https://docs.frigate.video/frigate/installation/">Frigate installation guide</a> explains the container volume mappings and hardware access.
            </p>
            <p>
                Home Assistant OS runs in a separate VM with its own USB controller assignment and Zigbee coordinator access. Its dashboards and configuration are versioned separately.
                Proxmox starts Frigate first and waits 120 seconds before requesting the Home Assistant start. Application checks after boot cover fresh Frigate recordings, Coral detection and a representative Home Assistant sensor update.
                The <a href="https://www.home-assistant.io/installation/alternative/">Home Assistant OS installation guide</a> provides the KVM/Proxmox disk image.
            </p>
            <pre><code class="language-bash"># On n2, inspect the two VM configurations and passthrough devices
qm config 100
qm config 105
lspci -nnk

# Inside the Frigate VM, from the Compose project directory
findmnt --mountpoint /mnt/frigate_storage
docker compose config --quiet
docker compose ps
docker compose logs --tail=30 frigate</code></pre>

            <h3 class="h5 mt-4">Media and tuner services</h3>
            <p>
                Plex and Immich on n3 depend on n1's SMB shares for media and photos. When n1 comes back after maintenance, the ZFS pools and NAS shares are checked before those applications resume reading and writing.
                On n1, Tvheadend uses a passed-through TBS PCIe tuner with driver modules built for the VM's Debian kernel. A guest kernel upgrade can require rebuilding those modules.
                After reboot, the appearance of the DVB adapters and a short recording to the NAS exercise the tuner, the guest and the storage path together.
            </p>

            <h2 class="h4 mt-4">Guest backups and recovery</h2>
            <p>
                Proxmox Backup Server runs in an LXC on n1, with a datastore on a dedicated SSD-backed ZFS pool. Scheduled jobs cover selected service guests across all three nodes.
                A recent successful backup is checked for accessibility and coverage before maintenance. A separate archive of the backup server's own container and a copy of n1's host configuration are held on another node so they can be retrieved while n1 is down.
            </p>
            <p>
                The guest backups cover selected virtual disks and container root filesystems. NAS media, Immich's photo bind mount and Frigate's separately passed-through recording disk sit outside those jobs.
                Checking a snapshot for integrity and restoring a guest from it are separate recovery checks. The <a href="https://pbs.proxmox.com/docs/maintenance.html">Proxmox Backup Server maintenance guide</a> describes pruning, garbage collection and verification jobs.
            </p>
            <pre><code class="language-bash"># From a Proxmox host while the backup datastore is available
pvesm status --storage pbs-ssd2
pvesm list pbs-ssd2 --vmid 105</code></pre>

            <h2 class="h4 mt-4">Rolling maintenance</h2>
            <p>
                Routine maintenance goes through n2, n3 and then n1, with one host offline at a time. Before updating a host, the other two nodes are checked for quorum, running guests are shut down cleanly,
                and services that depend on its storage are paused. Development workstation VMs are maintained separately and stay stopped after the host reboot.
                Home Assistant OS handles its own updates, and a Debian update inside the Frigate VM leaves the pinned Docker image at its existing version.
            </p>
            <pre><code class="language-bash"># On the host, after checking backups and stopping its guests
pveversion -v
apt update
apt --simulate full-upgrade

# After reviewing the proposed changes
apt full-upgrade

# After reboot
uname -r
pvecm status
pvesm status
systemctl --failed
qm list
pct list</code></pre>
            <p>
                The next node waits until the rebooted host has rejoined the cluster, its storage is available, and the intended guests and services have recovered.
                On n2 that includes camera feeds and home automation. On n3 it includes the media and photo mounts. On n1 the NAS shares and backup datastore come back before dependent services, followed by a Tvheadend recording check.
            </p>
